he's hot  blowing the pins in five  hallo the Master Chief collection is one  game but four halos that means four full  campaigns four games worth of  competitive multiplayer and a whole  bunch of Forge to boot it's a lot and as  a pure bang for your buck proposition  it's hard not to recommend jumping back  into a campaign is like traveling back  in time or opening a time capsule  depending on your age it takes a while  to get your bearings but once you're  dialed in all the thrills of yesteryear  come rushing back romping through levels  with a friend taking on the fierce  challenge of legendary difficulty  loading up that one level just to mess  around with vehicles all of these joys  are well preserved competitive  multiplayer is a different story once  you're in a playlist for a given game  and have gotten the hang of which pistol  you're using these matches can channel  the excitement and tension that so many  have enjoyed over the years  unfortunately serious matchmaking  problems make this scenario all but  unattainable as of this review developer  343 industries is working diligently to  get everything up and running but it's  unclear how long it will be until all  the included multiplayer options are  readily available and how many players  will stick around that long to populate  matches  these issues make the Master Chief  collection much more difficult to  recommend but there's still a lot to  enjoy here especially in Master Chief's  for galaxy spanning adventures between  them they span eleven years of Halo  demand to jump between campaigns is to  do a kind of developer assisted time  travel you're not changing controllers  or swapping discs and all the missions  are unlocked from the get-go so you're  free to mix and match easily from the  clear intuitive menus or use one of the  preset playlists that does the mixing  and matching for you it's a novel  feeling to have so much halo laid out  before you and the crisp organization of  it all serves as a 