hemselves looked pretty great too  especially in the earthbound levels  where the original visual design was a  bit more pedestrian switching between  the two instantly is a delight though  sticking with the anniversary look often  rewards you with environmental details  that simply weren't there before however  you'll also have to contend with more  extremes of light and dark the blinding  barrage of enemy artillery can  completely whiteout your screen while  the dark corners of buildings might make  you reach for your remote to up your  TV's brightness or switch to the  original visuals in hopes of  understanding where exactly you are  there are a few oddities in the  remastered audio as well  Miranda Keys sounds like she just took a  rip off a helium balloon yes sir though  honestly that's probably my memories of  Halo 3 Keys interfering because she  actually sounds similarly tinny in the  original Halo 2 cutscenes a few of the  updated weapon sounds made me yearn for  the originals but remastered visuals are  always paired with remastered sound so  there's no splitting the difference the  Brasher new SMG sounds disappointed me  personally but the new orchestration is  so good that I usually just stuck with  the remastered presentation  if we can Helmand sergeant extract the  chief and return to an Amber clad Roger  halo 3 is still pretty fantastic though  you'll likely wish blurr had taken a  whack at it when you see the original  cutscenes regardless of the lack of  remastering the game still looks pretty  solid given that it came out seven years  ago got a mold but the real joy of  playing halo 3 is the feeling that this  is the halo when Bungie really nailed  what halo was all about not just  designing levels that flow between  diverse areas in a pleasing cadence not  just making vehicles available on levels  that are playable on foot as well and  not just giving you a powerful yet  simple array of combat tools  what halo 3 does really well is  frequently create situations where you  have significant choice about how to  approach a combat scenario this  flexibility which was present to a  lesser degree in the previous games  allows playing solo playing co-op  playing on a harder difficulty playing  with skull modifiers on to all feel like  different endeavors you're not just  using your solo normal difficulty  strategies and executing them better  instead you're attempting daring stunts  when you're feeling confident and you're  experimenting with different approaches  when you're feeling overwhelmed halo 3  cultivates these moments and as a result  it feels like a challenge and it feels  like a playground which are some of the  best feelings that video games can  create  playing Halo 4 in the context of the  first Halo trilogy is to reinforce how  much it feels like a new chapter for the  series it's kind of shifting of gears  literally because Master Chief can now  sprint and narrative Lee because there  are new characters introduced and new  lore delved into with dialogue character  development and cinematography that is  the best the series had seen so far it  is distinctly of this era of gaming and  requires the least recalibration for  folks who have been keeping relatively  current with their shooters the  modernization of Master Chief's combat  mechanics doesn't sweep away the  traditional series strengths which are  still prominent here but rather it  updates them and makes him feel like a  proper super soldier only he's not the  only super soldier around anymore and  hearing people treat him with something  less than utter reverence signals the  start of a new trilogy as much as any  gameplay update or new enemy type  this new trilogy progressing next year  with a planned release of Halo 5  guardians also appears in other places  in the Master Chief collection entirely  new cutscenes for Halo 2 and a number of  new terminals to be found cover events  that will presumably become relevant in  a future release voiced as they are by  agent Locke the new protagonists for  Halo 5 these help contribute to the  cohesiveness of the collection as a  whole and engender the sense that this  nostalgic package is also  forward-looking  so there's a ton of action to sink your  energy sword into on the campaign side  and the same should be true for the  multiplayer side with four games worth  of competitive combat including both  classic and anniversary maps for Halo  two as well as halo combat Evolved  original action online on console for  the first time there's a lot of arenas  and game modes to get into combat  evolves vicious pistol halo 2's dual  wielding halo threes equipment and Halo  4's armor abilities are some of the  major differentiators but there are a  host of other mechanics balance tweaks  and visual changes that make each era  distinct hopping between them can feel  like a kind of whiplash but the through  lines that have kept Halo multiplayer  near the top of the heap shine through  diverse map design great vehicles and  versatile weapons all contribute to an  ever-shifting combat dynamic in which  your best strategy one minute might be  you're worse than next there's always  something you can do better when you  outmaneuver a tough opponent it's  sublime satisfaction at its best the  Master Chief collection is able to  recreate these moments but as of this  review it almost never does  getting into an online match can take  upwards of 10 minutes if it even works  at all once you're in you're often  confronted with lopsided teams and small  player count at the end of the match  you're usually left hanging on the  postgame report not put into a lobby to  join another match backing out and  re-entering a playlist sometimes causes  the game to crash it's a mess and the  developers have removed playlist options  while they work to issue improvements to  get this thing back on track despite  their frequent progress updates it's  unclear how long these issues will  persist 